We need to calculate the measure of the sides of the square. To do that, we first calculate the measure of the diagonal
$\overline{AC}$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
AC &= \sqrt{(x_{C} - x_{A})^{2} + (y_{C} - y_{A})^{2}} \\
&= \sqrt{(-1 - 1)^{2} + (3 - 2)^{2}} \\
&= \sqrt{4 + 1} \\
&= \sqrt{5}
\end{aligned}
$$

The diagonal $d$ and the side length $s$ of a square are related by the formula

$$
s = \dfrac{d}{\sqrt 2}
$$

In our case, we obtain

$$
s = \dfrac{AC}{\sqrt{2}} = \dfrac{\sqrt{5}}{\sqrt{2}}
$$

Therefore, the area of the square is

$$
\begin{aligned}
A &= s^{2} = (\frac{\sqrt{5}}{\sqrt{2}})^{2} = \frac{5}{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

